## Issue 7: No Environment Configuration

### ❌ BEFORE (Insecure)
```javascript
// VULNERABLE: Secrets hardcoded
const PORT = 3000;
const JWT_SECRET = 'my-secret-key'; // In source code!
const DATABASE_URL = 'postgresql://user:pass@localhost'; // Exposed!

app.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log('Server running');
});
```

**Risk**: 
- Secrets exposed in version control
- Same secret in all environments
- Difficult to change without redeploying

### ✅ AFTER (Secure)
```javascript
// SECURE: Use environment variables
require('dotenv').config();

const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;
const JWT_SECRET = process.env.JWT_SECRET;
const DATABASE_URL = process.env.DATABASE_URL;

// Validate required variables
if (process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production' && !JWT_SECRET) {
  throw new Error('JWT_SECRET environment variable is required');
}

app.listen(PORT, () => {
  logger.info(`Server running on port ${PORT}`);
});
```

**.env file** (NOT in git):
```bash
JWT_SECRET=<generated-secure-random-string>
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:pass@localhost
NODE_ENV=production
PORT=3000
```

**Benefits**:
- Secrets not in source code
- Different secrets per environment
- Easy to rotate secrets
- CI/CD friendly

