ATTACK #3: INFORMATION DISCLOSURE (Error Messages)
=================================================================

Scenario: Attacker triggers errors to learn about system internals

VULNERABLE CODE:
  app.get('/users/:id', (req, res) => {
    const user = db.query(`SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ${req.params.id}`);
    res.json(user);
    // No error handling - stack trace exposed
  });

ATTACK EXECUTION:
  $ curl "http://localhost:3000/users/abc123"

  // Error response might reveal:
  Error: connect ECONNREFUSED 127.0.0.1:27017
      at TCPConnectWrap.afterConnect [as oncomplete] (net.js:1141:18)
      at Database.query (/app/db.js:45:23)
      at /app/app.js:32:19
      ...

  // Attacker learns:
  ✓ Database type (MongoDB)
  ✓ Database host/port
  ✓ File paths and line numbers
  ✓ Technology stack

IMPACT:
  ✓ Reveals system architecture
  ✓ Helps plan more targeted attacks
  ✓ Identifies potential vulnerabilities
  ✓ Database location exposed

PREVENTION:
  app.get('/users/:id', (req, res) => {
    try {
      const user = db.query(`SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ${req.params.id}`);
      res.json(user);
    } catch (error) {
      // Generic message to client
      console.error('Database error:', error);
      res.status(500).json({ error: 'Server error' });
    }
  });

=================================================================
ATTACK #4: SQL INJECTION (If parameters were used)
=================================================================

Scenario: Attacker injects SQL code into parameters

VULNERABLE CODE:
  app.get('/users/:id', (req, res) => {
    const id = req.params.id;
    // VULNERABLE: Direct string interpolation
    db.query(`SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ${id}`, (err, result) => {
      res.json(result);
    });
  });

ATTACK EXECUTION:
  $ curl "http://localhost:3000/users/1 OR 1=1"

  // Executes: SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = 1 OR 1=1
  // Returns ALL users instead of just ID 1

  $ curl "http://localhost:3000/users/1; DROP TABLE users;--"

  // Executes multiple statements:
  // SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = 1
  // DROP TABLE users  <-- DELETES ENTIRE TABLE

  $ curl "http://localhost:3000/users/1 UNION SELECT password FROM admin;--"

  // Extracts passwords from admin table

IMPACT:
  ✓ Unauthorized data access
  ✓ Data modification
  ✓ Database destruction
  ✓ Admin account compromise
  ✓ System compromise

REAL-WORLD EXAMPLE:
  Sony PlayStation Network hack (2011)
  SQL injection + weak passwords
  Result: 77 million users affected, $171M settlement

PREVENTION:
  // Use parameterized queries
  db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?', [id], 
    (err, result) => {
      res.json(result);
    }
  );

=================================================================
ATTACK #5: NOSQL INJECTION (MongoDB)
=================================================================

Scenario: Attacker injects MongoDB operators

VULNERABLE CODE:
  app.get('/users', (req, res) => {
    const name = req.query.name;
    // VULNERABLE: Direct object construction
    db.users.find({ name: name }).toArray((err, users) => {
      res.json(users);
    });
  });

ATTACK EXECUTION:
