### Vulnerability 4: No Error Handling

#### ❌ ORIGINAL (VULNERABLE)
```javascript
app.get('/users', (req, res) => {
  res.json([...]);  // No error handling
});

// Stack trace exposed!
app.listen(3000, (err) => {
  if (err) console.log(err);  // Exposed to logs
});
```

**Problems:**
- Unhandled exceptions crash server
- Stack traces leak information
- No monitoring/logging
- Difficult to debug production issues

**Example Leak:**
```
Error: Cannot read property 'email' of undefined
    at /app/routes/users.js:15:20
    at Layer.handle [as handle_request]
    at next (/app/node_modules/express/lib/router.js:250:13)
    at /app/middleware/auth.js:5:13

Database connection string visible!
File paths visible!
```

#### ✅ SECURE VERSION
```javascript
// Global error handler
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  logger.error('Unhandled error:', {
    message: err.message,
    stack: err.stack,
    path: req.path,
    method: req.method,
    ip: req.ip
  });

  const status = err.status || 500;
  const isDevelopment = NODE_ENV === 'development';

  res.status(status).json({
    error: {
      message: isDevelopment ? err.message : 'Internal server error',
      status: status,
      ...(isDevelopment && { stack: err.stack })
    }
  });
});

// 404 handler
app.use((req, res) => {
  logger.warn(`404: ${req.method} ${req.path}`);
  res.status(404).json({ 
    error: 'Endpoint not found',
    message: `${req.method} ${req.path} does not exist`
  });
});
```

**Solutions:**
- All errors caught and handled
- Stack traces only in development
- Generic messages in production
- All events logged securely
- No sensitive data in error messages

---

### Vulnerability 5: No Rate Limiting

#### ❌ ORIGINAL (VULNERABLE)
```javascript
app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server running on port 3000');
});
```

**Problems:**
- No rate limiting
- Brute force attacks possible
- DoS attacks possible
- API abuse not prevented

**Attack:**
```bash
# Brute force login
for i in {1..10000}; do
  curl -X POST http://localhost:3000/login \
    -d 'username=admin&password='$i'&' &
done
